\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Paama, also called Paamese (ISO 639-3 \texttt{pma}, Glottocode paam1238), is an Austronesian language of Paama island, Vanuatu. About 6,000 speakers are reported. The main documentation is the work of Terry Crowley, both of whose books are openly hosted by the Australian National University (ANU).

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Paamese were found in the search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Crowley, T. (1982), \emph{The Paamese Language of Vanuatu}, Pacific Linguistics B-87 (grammar, full PDF at ANU): \url{https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/bitstream/1885/145173/1/PL-B87.pdf}
\item Crowley, T. (1992), \emph{A Dictionary of Paamese}, Pacific Linguistics C-121 (Paamese--English with an English--Paamese index, full PDF at ANU): \url{https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/bitstream/1885/145802/1/PL-C121.pdf}
\item Crowley's earlier work, ``The language of Paama (New Hebrides)'', at ANU: \url{https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/handle/1885/132944}
\item LINGUIST List notice for the dictionary: \url{https://linguistlist.org/issues/5/1263/}
\item Wikipedia article on Paamese: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paamese_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None were found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video or software resources were found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Paamese is described as stable, with all community members speaking it, but it is not known to be taught in schools. The grammar and dictionary give a solid base for developing a vernacular primer and word lists. No Paamese-language teaching materials or local education bodies were identified in the search results.
\end{document}
